The sense ”computer” is a {{clipping|sv|datamaskin|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|sv|/²dɑːta/}}
** {{IPA|sv|[ˈd̪ɑ̹ː˧˩ˌt̪a˥˩]|[ˈd͇ɑ̹ː˧˩ˌt̪a˥˩]|a=Central Sweden}}
* {{hyph|sv|da|ta}}

===Noun===
{{sv-noun|c}}

# {{lb|sv|uncountable}} information, especially encoded information that can be processed by computers
# {{lb|sv|colloquial|proscribed}} {{alternative form of|sv|dator|t=computer}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Det är fel på '''datan'''.|Something's wrong with '''the computer'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|sv|year=1966|author=Olof Johannesson (pen name of {{w|Hannes Alfvén}}) |title=Sagan om den stora datamaskinen |text=De första '''datorna''' var ju också mycket enkla. |t=The first '''computers''' were indeed very simple.}}

====Usage notes====
* The first definition is rarely inflected, but most often used in its basic form. In the definite form, both neuter ({{m|sv|datat}}) and common gender ({{m|sv|datan}}) forms are used. For the compound {{m|sv|indata}}, Google yields 440,000 hits, but only 2110 for {{m|sv|indatan}} and 1200 for {{m|sv|indatat}}. The Latin singular {{m|sv|datum}} is not used in this sense, because it is already used for ”date (in the calendar)”.
* Swedish lacked a good and short word for computer until {{m|sv|dator}} was proposed in 1967. The colloquial {{m|sv|data}} was used in the 1960s and is still used colloquially today, but is usually proscribed. The form {{m|sv|dator}} is also the plural of ''data'', and the plural definite forms {{m|sv|datorerna}}/{{m|sv|datorna}} are very similar.

